---
// For eBay sellers (Hashir, 17 Nov 2026): what OmniBridge reads and writes through the eBay APIs,
// how a seller connects and disconnects, account deletion notices and eBay's data rules (PRD 3.3,
// 7.3). Wording drafted by S2 from the PRD; the client reviews it.
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { CONTACT } from '../settings.ts';

const uses = [
  ['Orders, with the buyer’s name and delivery address', 'To dispatch orders and print shipping labels'],
  ['Tracking numbers (written to eBay)', 'So eBay and your buyer see the order as dispatched'],
  ['Stock quantity on your listings (written to eBay)', 'To keep one stock figure across eBay and Amazon'],
  ['Your active listings', 'To match your eBay listings to your products'],
  ['Transactions and payouts', 'To show profit per order and the VAT figures for your returns'],
  ['Buyer messages (read and sent)', 'So your team can answer buyers from one inbox'],
  ['Seller feedback (read and replied to)', 'To see and answer feedback'],
  ['Seller standards', 'To show your eBay account health'],
];
---

<Base
  title="For eBay sellers"
  description="How OmniBridge connects to your eBay seller account: what it reads and writes, how you connect and disconnect, account deletion and eBay's data rules."
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="page-head">
      <h1>OmniBridge for eBay sellers</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        How OmniBridge works with your eBay seller account: what it reads and writes, how you connect
        and disconnect, and how eBay data is protected.
      </p>
    </header>
    <div class="prose section-tail">
      <h2>What OmniBridge uses, and why</h2>
      <p>
        OmniBridge uses eBay’s APIs with your permission. It reads and writes only what the features
        below need.
      </p>
      <div class="table-scroll">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Data</th><th>Why</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {uses.map(([data, why]) => (
              <tr><td>{data}</td><td>{why}</td></tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2>Connecting your account</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>A workspace admin chooses the company and selects <strong>Connect eBay</strong>.</li>
        <li>
          You sign in on eBay’s own consent page and approve access there. OmniBridge never asks for,
          sees or stores your eBay password.
        </li>
        <li>If you decline on eBay’s page, nothing is connected and you can try again at any time.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Disconnecting</h2>
      <p>
        You can disconnect in OmniBridge or remove the app’s access in your eBay account settings.
        Changing your eBay password also ends OmniBridge’s access, and the account then shows as
        needing re-authorisation.
      </p>

      <h2>When an eBay account is closed</h2>
      <p>
        OmniBridge is subscribed to eBay’s account deletion notices. When eBay tells us a user has
        asked for their account to be deleted, we delete that user’s data from OmniBridge within 30
        days. The deletion cannot be undone.
      </p>

      <h2>eBay’s data rules</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>eBay data is never used to train or run machine learning or AI.</li>
        <li>OmniBridge does not compare your eBay performance with other channels.</li>
        <li>eBay data is never sent to any website.</li>
        <li>
          Buyers’ usernames and delivery details are kept only in a separate encrypted database in
          Germany, and deleted within 30 days of delivery.
        </li>
        <li>If eBay ends our API access, all eBay data is destroyed within 10 days.</li>
        <li>
          Every user of a workspace agrees to eBay’s terms for “Your Users” in our <a href="/terms"
            >terms of service</a
          >.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Questions</h2>
      <p>
        Email <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT.support}`}>{CONTACT.support}</a>, or
        <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT.privacy}`}>{CONTACT.privacy}</a> for data requests. Full details are
        in our <a href="/privacy">privacy policy</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
